    summary_en: "A cybersecurity researcher has disclosed a memory corruption vulnerability, CVE-2026-64531 (CVSS score 7.8), in the Linux kernel's Open vSwitch (OVS) data path, codenamed OVSwrap. The vulnerability stems from an overflow in the OVS length field. When an unprivileged user sends a specially crafted Netlink attribute via a namespace, it triggers memory corruption and overwrites kernel credentials, thereby achieving system Root privileges. Multiple Linux distributions with default configurations, including Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora, and AlmaLinux, are affected. A public PoC has demonstrated exploitation across nearly 800 kernel versions. Official patches have been released (e.g., 5.15.212, 6.1.178, etc). If immediate updating is not possible, it is recommended to blacklist the openvswitch module load or restrict unprivileged user namespaces.",
